# Maven Entry Pilot Proposal

The previous version put Maven vanilla calculator entry and Renamed Gradle starter result on the same migration checklist, which made discussion easier but obscured the difference between the two types of evidence. After this review, the Maven side continues to enter through the consumer test directory; the Gradle side can still only be confirmed as a caller result. The two sides no longer share the judgment “the example passes.”

The review still points to `junit5-maven-consumer/src/test/java/com/example/project/FirstTest.java`. The visible file header is:

```java
package com.example.project;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.DisplayName;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Tag;
```

These declarations show that this location is adjacent to the JUnit Jupiter test side: assertions, display names, and tags all appear here. They do not put the Gradle starter's name or result in the file header, so this test file cannot be treated as proof of the Gradle rename. The earlier claim that “the same calculator sample can cover both sides” is withdrawn.

The pilot scope is now two short paths. The first retains the normal Maven test entry and uses the calculator addition sample as the observation point for whether the entry still works. The second records the actual version, entry point, and result only after finding a caller that uses Renamed Gradle starter result; without that caller, no conclusion is fabricated just to fill the migration item.

The tradeoff here is reduced superficial symmetry. Having the Gradle rename follow the Maven test looks easier, but once the caller's startup layout differs, stable assertions on the Maven side still cannot answer whether the renamed starter was adopted correctly. Conversely, the Gradle result cannot vouch for the Maven consumer test entry.

The withdrawal conditions are correspondingly narrower: if the Maven path needs an additional entry to display the calculator example, it should not be called a vanilla path; if the Gradle item has no verifiable user, leave it pending investigation rather than close it with the Maven result. The next supplement should add only the objects left by the actual review, without expanding to other build entries.

# Ant Calculator Result Proposal

The migration review provisionally treats Ant starter calculator result as a result name to investigate, without equating it with a passing record for any particular consumer test. The earlier investigation trail ended at `junit5-maven-consumer/src/test/java/com/example/project/FirstTest.java`. The file header shows:

    package com.example.project;
    import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;
    import org.junit.jupiter.api.DisplayName;
    import org.junit.jupiter.api.Tag;

This lead is a suitable starting point for checking the consumer-side calculator assertions and interface shape. It cannot, on its own, establish what the Ant starter actually executed.

The proposal is to retain two narrowly scoped attempts. One retrieves Ant starter records from an existing automation job and records the job, commit, and actual artifact name; the other follows `FirstTest.java` to inspect calls near the assertions, recording only what the test file can confirm. If the job records cannot tie these three items together, the review will not substitute consumer test results for starter results.

Retract if subsequent checks confirm that this path is only an old navigation lead, not the direct execution location for Ant. On retraction, retain the test-side observations and remove claims that infer starter results from this file.
